      {
        "section_id": "nav:jamieson-fausset-brown:PSA.69:P:psa-69-1-36-upon-shoshannim-see-on-psa-45-1-title-mingling-the-language-of-pra",
        "navigation_id": "PSA.69:P:psa-69-1-36-upon-shoshannim-see-on-psa-45-1-title-mingling-the-language-of-pra",
        "scope": "passage",
        "role": "passage_overview",
        "title": "Psa 69:1-36 . Upon Shoshannim--(See on Psa 45:1 , title). Mingling the language of prayer and complaint, the sufferer, whose condition is here set forth, pleads for God's help as one suffering in His cause, implores the divine retribution on his malicious enemies, and, viewing his deliverance as sure, promises praise by himself, and others, to whom God will extend like blessings. This Psalm is referred to seven times in the New Testament as prophetical of Christ and the gospel times. Although the character in which the Psalmist appears to some in Psa 69:5 is that of a sinner, yet his condition as a sufferer innocent of alleged crimes sustains the typical character of the composition, and it may be therefore regarded throughout, as the twenty-second, as typically expressive of the feelings of our Saviour in the flesh."
      }
    ],
